        'Know Your Rights as a Student' => <<<'HTML'
<p>College students have important protections, but there is no single nationwide “student bill of rights” that answers every problem. Rights may come from federal, state, or local law; a school’s public or private status; an employment or housing relationship; accreditation and licensing rules; and the catalog, handbook, enrollment agreement, or other school policies. Start by identifying the rule that applies and the deadline for using it.</p>

<h2>Know the major federal protections</h2>
<p>Schools receiving federal funds generally may not discriminate on grounds covered by federal education civil-rights laws, including race, color, national origin, sex, disability, and age. The precise law, coverage, and remedy differ. Retaliation for asserting a protected civil-rights concern is also prohibited. Title IX addresses sex discrimination; Section 504 and the ADA address disability access; FERPA governs access to and disclosure of education records.</p>

<h2>Understand what depends on the school</h2>
<p>Public institutions are government actors and may have constitutional obligations that do not apply in the same way to private institutions. State law can add protections. Religious institutions, military schools, tribal institutions, professional programs, and students acting as employees may be subject to additional or different rules. A school procedure can promise protections beyond the federal minimum, but it does not override controlling law.</p>

<h2>Expect notice and published information, not a guaranteed outcome</h2>
<p>Read the current handbook, catalog, course syllabus, conduct code, housing agreement, and applicable appeal policy. Many rights are procedural: a chance to inspect a record, request an accommodation, receive required information, or use an appeal. They do not guarantee that a grade changes, a sanction disappears, or a requested accommodation is approved exactly as proposed.</p>

<h2>Protect privacy and access</h2>
<p>At the postsecondary level, FERPA rights ordinarily belong to the student, even when a parent pays tuition. FERPA has exceptions and is not a promise that every campus conversation is confidential. Students with disabilities generally must identify the barrier and request an academic adjustment through the school’s designated process; an old IEP does not automatically carry into college.</p>

<h2>Know the safety and consumer-information routes</h2>
<p>Schools covered by the Clery Act publish campus security policies and crime statistics. Institutions participating in federal student aid must make specified information available about aid, costs, programs, completion, campus safety, and complaint contacts. For an immediate threat, call 911 or campus emergency services rather than waiting for an administrative process.</p>

<h2>Preserve your ability to act</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Save the policy version, syllabus, notices, emails, forms, and a dated timeline.</li>
  <li>Identify the exact decision, the stated reason, the requested remedy, and the appeal deadline.</li>
  <li>Ask for instructions in writing and submit through the required channel.</li>
  <li>Do not assume an informal conversation pauses an internal or external deadline.</li>
  <li>Use confidential support, an ombuds office, legal aid, or a lawyer when the consequences are serious.</li>
</ol>

<hr><h2>Official starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws">U.S. Department of Education: civil-rights laws</a></li>
