        'FERPA Explained' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) is a federal law governing education records at schools that receive applicable U.S. Department of Education funds. It gives eligible students rights to inspect records, request correction of certain problems, control many disclosures, and complain about alleged violations. It is not a universal confidentiality law and does not replace a grade appeal.</p>

<h2>Who holds the rights?</h2>
<p>FERPA rights transfer from the parent to the student when the student turns 18 or attends a postsecondary institution at any age. At college, the student is usually the rights holder. A school may disclose information to a parent under a FERPA exception—for example, in some dependency or health-and-safety circumstances—but parent access is not automatic merely because the parent pays the bill.</p>

<h2>What is an education record?</h2>
<p>Generally, it is information directly related to a student that the institution or a party acting for it maintains. Transcripts, advising records, disciplinary records, and identifiable electronic records may qualify. FERPA has exclusions, including certain sole-possession notes, law-enforcement-unit records, and employee records when employment is not dependent on student status. Whether a particular item is covered depends on how it was created and maintained.</p>

<h2>Inspection and amendment rights</h2>
<p>Ask the registrar or FERPA office how to inspect records. A school generally must comply with a proper request within 45 days, although FERPA does not always require a free copy. You may request amendment of information you believe is inaccurate, misleading, or otherwise violates FERPA privacy rights and may have a hearing if the school refuses.</p>
<p>The amendment procedure is not a way to relitigate academic judgment. It can correct a record that inaccurately reports a grade, but a claim that the instructor should have awarded a different grade belongs in the school’s grade-appeal process.</p>

<h2>Consent and important exceptions</h2>
<p>A school ordinarily needs signed, dated consent identifying the records, purpose, and recipients, but FERPA permits disclosures without consent in defined circumstances. Examples can include school officials with legitimate educational interests, transfer to another school, financial-aid functions, certain subpoenas or orders, and a health-or-safety emergency. An exception permits a disclosure; it does not make every disclosure appropriate.</p>

<h2>Directory information and access permissions</h2>
<p>A school may designate categories such as name or enrollment status as directory information after giving notice and an opt-out opportunity. Find the annual notice and opt-out deadline. If you authorize a parent or supporter through a portal, clarify exactly what the authorization covers; billing access, FERPA consent, and legal authority are not necessarily the same.</p>

<h2>How to raise a concern</h2>
<p>Document the record or disclosure, date, people involved, and requested correction. Contact the registrar, privacy officer, or office listed in the annual FERPA notice. A complaint to the U.S. Department of Education’s Student Privacy Policy Office must generally allege a timely violation—normally within 180 days of the violation or when you knew or reasonably should have known about it. An internal conversation may not extend that deadline.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/family-educational-rights-and-privacy-act">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA overview</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/faq/are-educational-agencies-and-institutions-required-notify-parents-and-eligible-students-their">Student Privacy Policy Office: required annual notice</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/file-a-complaint">Student Privacy Policy Office: file a FERPA complaint</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice; consult your school’s current FERPA notice and records policy.</em></p>
