        'Title IX Basics' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Title IX is a federal civil-rights law prohibiting sex discrimination in education programs or activities receiving federal financial assistance. It can apply to sex-based harassment and sexual violence, pregnancy or related conditions, athletics, admissions where covered, and other unequal treatment. Other federal or state laws and school policies may provide additional protections.</p>

<h2>Use the current federal baseline</h2>
<p>As of this guide’s review, a federal court vacated the 2024 Title IX regulations nationwide in January 2025. The U.S. Department of Education states that the 2020 Title IX regulations remain in effect. Online summaries written for the vacated 2024 rule may therefore be wrong. Check the Department’s current regulations page and the date of your school’s policy.</p>

<h2>Find the Title IX coordinator</h2>
<p>A covered institution identifies a Title IX coordinator who receives reports, explains available supportive measures and complaint options, and coordinates compliance. A report tells the school about a concern; under the applicable policy, a formal complaint or grievance step may be required to begin a particular adjudication. Ask what information will be shared, who is a mandatory reporter under school policy, and what confidential resources are available before disclosing details.</p>

<h2>Support and emergency help</h2>
<p>Supportive measures can address access to education while a matter is assessed, such as academic flexibility, no-contact arrangements, housing or schedule changes, safety planning, or referrals. Availability and terminology vary. For immediate danger or urgent medical needs, contact 911, campus emergency services, or a medical provider. A confidential advocate or counselor can help you consider reporting options without deciding for you.</p>

<h2>Understand the applicable process</h2>
<p>Title IX coverage and the grievance procedure depend on the alleged conduct, location and program connection, dates, parties, and current law. School conduct rules may address behavior even when it falls outside the federal Title IX grievance process. Request the exact policy, definitions, standard of evidence, advisor rules, timeline, evidence-review steps, possible outcomes, and appeal grounds. Do not assume a criminal case and a school case use the same rules or timing.</p>

<h2>Pregnancy, athletics, and other sex discrimination</h2>
<p>Title IX is broader than sexual misconduct. Contact the coordinator about discrimination related to pregnancy or related conditions, unequal access to programs, or sex-based treatment in athletics and academics. Disability, race, national-origin, or employment laws may overlap. Retaliation for participating in or asserting rights under laws enforced by the Department’s Office for Civil Rights (OCR) is prohibited.</p>

<h2>External complaints and deadlines</h2>
<p>You may contact OCR about discrimination by a covered school. OCR states that a complaint ordinarily must be filed within 180 calendar days of the last alleged discrimination. You generally do not have to complete the school grievance process first, but using it can affect OCR timing rules, so read the instructions carefully. OCR is a neutral fact finder and does not act as your personal lawyer.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/title-ix-and-sex-discrimination">U.S. Department of Education: Title IX</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/federal-register-notices-and-regulations">U.S. Department of Education: current Title IX regulations</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/file-complaint/how-file-discrimination-complaint-ocr">Office for Civil Rights: how to file a complaint</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/retaliation/retaliation-discrimination">Office for Civil Rights: retaliation</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. Current federal and state law and your institution’s applicable policy control.</em></p>
