        'Grade Appeals Explained' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A grade appeal is a request to review whether a course grade was determined and recorded according to the applicable academic rules. It is not normally a second opinion about the quality of the student’s work. Colleges generally give instructors substantial responsibility for academic evaluation, while their policies may allow correction of defined errors or improper departures from stated standards.</p>

<h2>Verify the grade first</h2>
<p>Recalculate the result using the syllabus weights, dropped assignments, attendance rules, extra credit, rounding policy, and grades shown on returned work. Distinguish an LMS running total from the official final grade. Ask the instructor a neutral, specific question and include your calculation. A data-entry or arithmetic mistake can often be corrected without a formal appeal.</p>

<h2>Find the allowed grounds</h2>
<p>Policies often limit appeals to matters such as a calculation or recording error; use of criteria inconsistent with the syllabus or applied differently without an academic reason; arbitrary or capricious treatment; a material procedural failure; or discrimination prohibited by policy or law. The exact wording matters. “I worked hard,” “I need the grade,” or “another instructor might grade differently” normally does not establish a listed ground.</p>

<h2>Use the correct sequence</h2>
<p>The route may begin with the instructor and move to a chair, dean, or faculty committee. If contacting the instructor is unsafe, impossible, or excused by policy, use the alternate route. Note the filing window; it may be only a few days or weeks after grades post. Keep communication factual and do not miss the deadline while waiting for an informal reply.</p>

<h2>Present relevant evidence</h2>
<p>Submit the syllabus, grading rubric, returned work, feedback, gradebook entries, relevant messages, and a concise calculation or timeline. Identify the policy ground and connect each document to it. Request a remedy within the reviewer’s authority—often correction, reconsideration under the stated criteria, or review by another authorized person—not an automatic preferred grade.</p>

<h2>Keep FERPA and disability issues separate</h2>
<p>FERPA amendment rights can correct a record that inaccurately reports the grade actually awarded; they do not challenge the instructor’s substantive academic judgment. Likewise, an accommodation request and a grade appeal can be related but use different processes. Accommodations are not presumed retroactive, yet a failure to implement an approved accommodation should be raised promptly with disability services and through any grievance route.</p>

<h2>After the decision</h2>
<p>Read whether a further appeal exists and which grounds it accepts, such as new evidence, conflict of interest, or procedural error. Save the written outcome and updated transcript. If the concern involves prohibited discrimination or retaliation, consult the civil-rights office and external deadlines; an ordinary grade disagreement alone is not a federal civil-rights complaint.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/family-educational-rights-and-privacy-act">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/faq/are-educational-agencies-and-institutions-required-notify-parents-and-eligible-students-their">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA rights notice</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws">U.S. Department of Education: civil-rights laws</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice; your course and institution’s grade-appeal policy control.</em></p>
