        'Academic Misconduct Hearings' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An academic misconduct hearing considers an allegation such as unauthorized collaboration, plagiarism, cheating, fabrication, prohibited assistance, or another violation of the school’s academic-integrity rules. Names and procedures vary widely. The applicable code, not courtroom television or another college’s website, explains the student’s process.</p>

<h2>Read the allegation and policy</h2>
<p>Identify the specific rule, conduct, assignment or exam, date, evidence described, possible academic and disciplinary consequences, and response deadline. Check which policy version applies and whether the matter is handled by the instructor, an integrity officer, a panel, or more than one process. A failing assignment grade and a conduct sanction may have separate appeals.</p>

<h2>Preserve information lawfully</h2>
<p>Keep the syllabus, assignment instructions, drafts, version history, citations, notes, permitted-tool instructions, messages, and original files with metadata intact. Write a contemporaneous timeline. Do not alter documents, coordinate stories, pressure witnesses, access another person’s account, or destroy material. Respect privacy restrictions when sharing case records.</p>

<h2>Understand your procedural options</h2>
<p>The notice should point to the response or hearing process. Determine whether you may review evidence, submit a written statement, identify witnesses, challenge a conflict, bring an advisor, ask questions, or request accessibility or language support. These are policy-dependent; an advisor may be allowed but unable to speak. Ask questions early and in writing.</p>

<h2>Prepare a clear response</h2>
<p>Address each allegation separately. State what happened, what instructions you understood, what sources or tools you used, and which evidence supports the account. Acknowledge undisputed facts without guessing. If the issue involved an unclear collaboration or AI rule, quote the exact instruction and describe your workflow; do not assume ambiguity automatically defeats the allegation.</p>

<h2>At the meeting or hearing</h2>
<p>Arrive on time, have an organized copy of the record, answer the question asked, and distinguish memory from inference. Remain respectful even when disagreeing. Ask how the decision will be made, what standard of proof the policy uses, when written findings will issue, and whether interim restrictions remain in place. Request a reasonable accommodation through the designated office as early as possible.</p>

<h2>Outcome and appeal</h2>
<p>Read the findings, rationale, sanctions, transcript consequences, educational requirements, and appeal instructions. Appeals commonly have short deadlines and limited grounds, such as procedural error, new evidence, conflict, or sanction issues. A FERPA request is not a substitute for the academic-integrity appeal. If discrimination or retaliation affected the process, preserve the internal deadline while consulting the appropriate civil-rights channel.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/family-educational-rights-and-privacy-act">Student Privacy Policy Office: education-record privacy</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws">U.S. Department of Education: civil-rights protections</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/retaliation/retaliation-discrimination">Office for Civil Rights: retaliation</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. The academic-integrity code and notice for your case control.</em></p>
