        'Student Conduct Processes' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A student conduct process addresses alleged violations of a college’s code, such as disruptive behavior, harassment, safety violations, property damage, hazing, alcohol or drug violations, or misuse of technology. It is an administrative school process, not necessarily a criminal or civil court case. Procedures and protections differ by institution, jurisdiction, status, and allegation.</p>

<h2>Confirm jurisdiction and the rule</h2>
<p>Read the conduct code that applied on the incident date. Schools may claim jurisdiction over campus conduct, school activities, online behavior, or some off-campus behavior affecting the community. Identify the exact charge, definition, reporting party, date, location, and possible sanctions. Ask which office and decision maker are handling it.</p>

<h2>Separate parallel processes</h2>
<p>The same event may involve student conduct, Title IX, housing, athletics, employment, professional-program fitness, or law enforcement. Each can have different evidence rules and deadlines. A school case may continue despite a police report or court case. Before sharing information with overlapping processes, consider consulting an advisor or lawyer, especially when criminal exposure is possible.</p>

<h2>Review notice and interim actions</h2>
<p>Determine what response is required and by when. Interim steps—such as a no-contact directive, housing change, activity restriction, or emergency removal—are not necessarily final findings, but violating them can create a new issue. Follow the order while using any prompt review or challenge procedure stated in policy.</p>

<h2>Prepare the record</h2>
<p>Preserve messages, photos, access records, witness names, receipts, and a dated chronology without editing or posting them publicly. Review the school’s evidence-access, advisor, witness, confidentiality, recording, and conflict rules. Submit only relevant material and explain what it shows. Do not retaliate, intimidate, or ask others to coordinate accounts.</p>

<h2>Participate effectively</h2>
<p>At an informal resolution meeting or hearing, listen to the allegation, respond factually, and ask how disputed evidence will be evaluated. An informal resolution may involve accepting responsibility or waiving a hearing; understand the written terms before agreeing. Request disability, language, or other process access support early through the appropriate office.</p>

<h2>Decision, sanctions, and appeal</h2>
<p>Look for written findings, the standard applied, sanctions, completion deadlines, transcript or registration effects, and appeal instructions. Sanctions can range from education or warning to suspension or expulsion. Appeals are usually limited and time-sensitive. FERPA governs many maintained conduct records but contains disclosure exceptions; it does not make the process secret from everyone.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/content/family-educational-rights-and-privacy-act">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/title-ix-and-sex-discrimination">U.S. Department of Education: Title IX</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. The current code and case notice control your process.</em></p>
