        'Disability Accommodations: How They Work' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Disability accommodations—often called academic adjustments, auxiliary aids, or reasonable modifications—are changes that provide a qualified student with a disability meaningful access to a postsecondary program. Section 504 applies to covered recipients of federal funds, and the ADA applies according to its titles and coverage. Decisions are individualized; a diagnosis alone does not create an automatic menu of accommodations.</p>

<h2>College is different from K–12</h2>
<p>Colleges generally do not identify students or create an IEP for them. The student must notify the designated disability or accessibility office, follow its request process, and describe the barrier. An IEP or 504 plan can be useful documentation but does not automatically continue or bind the college.</p>

<h2>What an individualized review considers</h2>
<p>The office considers whether the student has a covered disability, how it affects access in the relevant setting, and which adjustment would be effective. A school may request reasonable documentation when disability or need is not obvious. Documentation standards should be reasonable and relevant; ask what alternative information is accepted if obtaining a new evaluation creates a barrier.</p>

<h2>Common adjustments</h2>
<p>Depending on individual need and context, adjustments may include testing changes, accessible materials or technology, interpreters or captioning, note-taking support, accessible classrooms, modified attendance or deadline arrangements, priority registration, or a reduced course load. The effective option need not be the student’s preferred option if another provides access.</p>

<h2>Limits and essential requirements</h2>
<p>A college need not lower essential academic standards, fundamentally alter a program, or provide an adjustment that creates an undue burden under the applicable law. It also does not generally provide personal attendants or personal devices for individual use. A conclusion that a requirement is essential should come from an appropriate individualized and academic review, not a stereotype about disability.</p>

<h2>Implementation is a shared process</h2>
<p>After approval, follow instructions for sending accommodation notices and scheduling tests or services. Faculty commonly receive the approved adjustments, not full medical documentation. Discuss logistics early without being required to disclose a diagnosis to classmates. Approval does not necessarily apply retroactively, and accommodations can require advance arrangements.</p>

<h2>When access is not working</h2>
<p>Contact the disability office promptly if an instructor, platform, placement site, or facility is not implementing an approved adjustment or if it is ineffective. Ask for an alternative, an interim measure, and the grievance or appeal procedure. The school should not charge the student for an approved auxiliary aid or academic adjustment. OCR also accepts qualifying disability-discrimination complaints, ordinarily subject to a 180-day filing window.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: students with disabilities preparing for postsecondary education</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-academic-adjustments-postsecondary-students">Office for Civil Rights: postsecondary academic adjustments</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/individuals-disabilities/auxiliary-aids-and-services-for-postsecondary-students-with-disabilities">U.S. Department of Education: auxiliary aids and services</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ada.gov/law-and-regs/ada/">U.S. Department of Justice: ADA law and regulations</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal or medical advice. Eligibility and effective adjustments require an individualized review.</em></p>
