        'Requesting Academic Accommodations' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Requesting an academic accommodation starts an individualized access process with the college’s disability or accessibility office. Do it as early as practical—ideally before placement tests, course registration, clinical work, housing selection, or exams—because arranging captioning, accessible materials, testing space, interpreters, or equipment can take time.</p>

<h2>Find the designated office</h2>
<p>Search the school website and handbook for disability services, accessibility services, the Section 504 coordinator, or ADA coordinator. Complete the school’s request form if one exists. A conversation with an instructor, admission office, or advisor may not formally notify the office responsible for approving adjustments.</p>

<h2>Describe barriers and needs</h2>
<p>Explain the relevant functional limitations and how course design, testing, communication, technology, facilities, fieldwork, attendance, or scheduling creates a barrier. You do not need to propose the perfect solution, but prior effective accommodations can be helpful. Request access for all relevant settings, not only classroom exams.</p>

<h2>Provide appropriate documentation</h2>
<p>The school may ask for reasonable documentation when disability or need is not obvious. Useful information may include a qualified professional’s assessment, past accommodations, an IEP or 504 plan, testing, treatment information focused on function, or other credible records. Ask for the written documentation criteria and alternatives; requirements should relate to the requested adjustment.</p>

<h2>Participate in the interactive review</h2>
<p>Meet with the office, answer relevant questions, and discuss effective options. The approved adjustment may differ from the requested one if an alternative provides meaningful access. If the school says an adjustment would alter an essential requirement or create an undue burden, ask for the decision, basis, and available effective alternatives in writing.</p>

<h2>Activate approved accommodations</h2>
<p>Follow the process for accommodation letters, test booking, accessible-format requests, notetaking, or communication services each term. Give faculty reasonable notice and discuss logistics privately. Keep the approval and communications. Faculty should not require detailed medical records, but students remain responsible for meeting essential course requirements with the approved access measures.</p>

<h2>Address delays or denials quickly</h2>
<p>If access is delayed, denied, or not implemented, contact the disability office immediately and request an interim measure where appropriate. Use the institution’s disability grievance or appeal procedure and preserve course deadlines. OCR accepts disability-discrimination complaints, ordinarily within 180 days of the alleged discrimination; an internal process does not always stop that clock.</p>

<h2>Plan for changes</h2>
<p>Contact the office when disability effects, treatment, course format, clinical placement, or technology changes. Temporary conditions may qualify depending on their effects and duration. Do not assume accommodations are retroactive or automatically renew; verify the institution’s process before each term.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/higher-education/students-disabilities-preparing-postsecondary-education">U.S. Department of Education: preparing for postsecondary education</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-academic-adjustments-postsecondary-students">Office for Civil Rights: academic adjustments</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/individuals-disabilities/auxiliary-aids-and-services-for-postsecondary-students-with-disabilities">U.S. Department of Education: auxiliary aids and services</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal or medical advice. Use your institution’s current accessibility process.</em></p>
