        'Medical Leave / Leave of Absence Explained' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A medical leave or leave of absence is an approved pause from enrollment under a school’s policy. It is not the same as missing class, receiving incompletes, dropping every course, or requesting disability accommodations. Before signing anything, get the leave dates, academic status, charges, aid treatment, return requirements, and deadlines in writing.</p>

<h2>Compare all available paths</h2>
<p>Ask whether short-term absence support, incompletes, a reduced course load, course withdrawals, remote participation where available, disability adjustments, or formal leave could address the situation. A medical condition may be a disability depending on its effects. A school should make an individualized assessment rather than assume that leave is always the only safe or appropriate response.</p>

<h2>Use the official process</h2>
<p>Contact the dean of students, registrar, disability office, or leave coordinator named in policy. Determine who approves leave, what documentation is necessary, the effective date, maximum duration, status during leave, and whether the leave is voluntary. In an emergency, prioritize immediate care and have a trusted person ask the school how to preserve options.</p>

<h2>Understand academic consequences</h2>
<p>Ask how courses will appear—withdrawal marks, incompletes, grades, or removal—and whether program progression, catalog year, time-to-degree limits, research, clinical placements, licensure reporting, or readmission will change. Stopping attendance without an approved leave can produce failing grades or an unofficial withdrawal.</p>

<h2>Check billing and financial aid separately</h2>
<p>The school’s tuition-refund policy and federal Return of Title IV Funds calculation are different. A leave bearing that name in campus policy may still count as a withdrawal for federal-aid purposes unless it satisfies specific Title IV requirements. The school may have to return aid, leaving a balance owed. Ask financial aid and student accounts for written estimates before the effective date when possible.</p>

<h2>Review everything tied to enrollment</h2>
<p>Leave can affect loan in-school status and grace periods, scholarships, health insurance, housing and meal plans, campus employment, athletics, veterans benefits, immigration status, childcare, and access to email, libraries, labs, or healthcare. International students should consult the designated school official before reducing enrollment or leaving the United States; immigration consequences require individualized advice.</p>

<h2>Plan the return before leaving</h2>
<p>Obtain the earliest return term, notice deadline, registration priority, documentation or individualized assessment required, housing and aid applications, and what happens if more time is needed. Return criteria should be connected to readiness to participate safely and meet essential requirements, not demand that a person be symptom-free. Ask how to challenge a denial or request reasonable modifications.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-specific-illnesses-and-medical-conditions">Office for Civil Rights: illnesses, medical conditions, and disability</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2026-2027/vol5/ch1-general-requirements-withdrawals-and-return-title-iv-funds">Federal Student Aid: withdrawals and Return of Title IV Funds</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/manage-loans/repayment/prepare-payments-student-loans">Federal Student Aid: prepare for loan repayment</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal, financial, immigration, or medical advice. Obtain individualized answers before changing enrollment.</em></p>
