        'Withdrawing From College' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Withdrawing from college means ending enrollment in all courses for a term or leaving the institution under its official process. It is different from dropping one class, taking an approved leave, or simply no longer attending. An unofficial departure can create failing grades, unexpected debt, and unresolved housing or loan obligations.</p>

<h2>Act before disappearing</h2>
<p>Find the registrar or designated withdrawal office and ask for the form, deadline, effective-date rule, required approvals, and confirmation. If health or safety prevents you from completing the process personally, ask about an authorized representative or emergency procedure. Keep a submitted copy and written effective date.</p>

<h2>Check the academic record</h2>
<p>Ask whether courses will be removed, marked with withdrawals, or assigned grades at the intended date. Review program dismissal rules, catalog-year limits, incomplete options, reentry or readmission requirements, and transcript holds permitted under applicable rules. Speak with an advisor about alternatives if withdrawing would delay a sequence or licensure program.</p>

<h2>Separate school refunds from federal aid</h2>
<p>A tuition-refund schedule determines what institutional charges are reduced. The federal Return of Title IV Funds calculation determines how much federal aid was earned. They are separate. For many withdrawals at or before the 60-percent point of a payment period, aid is earned proportionally; after that point, 100 percent is generally earned, though the school still completes the calculation and its own refund policy still applies. Returned aid can create a new balance owed to the school.</p>

<h2>Review loans, grants, and scholarships</h2>
<p>Ask financial aid for a written estimate and whether a post-withdrawal disbursement, grant repayment, satisfactory academic progress issue, or scholarship adjustment applies. Dropping below half-time or withdrawing can change federal loan in-school status and start or use a grace period. Complete required exit counseling and update contact information with the loan servicer.</p>

<h2>Close related obligations</h2>
<p>Contact housing, dining, health insurance, campus employment, library, parking, athletics, veterans-benefits, and international-student offices as applicable. Return equipment and keys. International students must speak with their designated school official before withdrawing because a loss of enrollment can affect status and departure timing.</p>

<h2>Create a reentry plan</h2>
<p>Obtain the readmission application, deadlines, conditions, program-capacity rules, balance requirements, and future aid steps. Ask whether completed credits expire or whether the catalog changes after an absence. Save the catalog, degree audit, transcript, withdrawal confirmation, account statement, aid calculation, and contact names.</p>

<h2>If the deadline has passed</h2>
<p>Ask about late or retroactive withdrawal, medical withdrawal, or an academic appeal. Approval is not automatic and documentation rules vary. State the requested effective date, reason the normal deadline could not be met, and the remedy sought. Continue monitoring your account until every decision and balance is final.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2026-2027/vol5/ch1-general-requirements-withdrawals-and-return-title-iv-funds">Federal Student Aid: withdrawals and Return of Title IV Funds</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2026-2027/vol5/ch2-steps-return-title-iv-aid-calculation-part-1">Federal Student Aid: Return of Title IV Funds calculation</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/exit-counseling/">Federal Student Aid: exit counseling</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not financial or legal advice. Obtain written estimates based on your school, term, aid, and withdrawal date.</em></p>
