        'How to File a Complaint With Your School' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A strong complaint identifies a specific action, the rule or expectation involved, supporting facts, and a realistic requested remedy. It also goes to the office with authority to act. “Complaint” is not one universal procedure: grades, disability access, discrimination, financial aid, conduct, employment, research, housing, and consumer concerns may use different channels.</p>

<h2>Address urgent risk first</h2>
<p>For immediate danger, call 911 or campus emergency services. For urgent health needs, seek medical care. Use confidential advocacy or counseling if desired, but ask about confidentiality limits before sharing identifying details. Emergency action does not necessarily file an academic or administrative complaint.</p>

<h2>Define the issue and remedy</h2>
<p>Write one sentence describing the decision or conduct, date, people or office involved, and impact. Then state what you want the school to do: explain a decision, correct a record, provide access, reconsider under policy, stop conduct, refund a charge, or investigate. Separate firsthand facts from assumptions and avoid unrelated accusations.</p>

<h2>Choose the right internal channel</h2>
<p>Use the handbook’s named office: instructor or chair for many academic issues; registrar for records; financial aid or student accounts for aid and billing; disability services or the 504/ADA coordinator for access; Title IX or equal-opportunity office for discrimination; student conduct for code matters; human resources for student-employment concerns; and the dean of students or ombuds office for navigation. An ombuds office may be confidential and informal but often cannot reverse a decision.</p>

<h2>Submit a usable record</h2>
<p>Include a concise chronology, relevant policy language, important documents, steps already taken, and requested resolution. Redact unnecessary sensitive information and use the secure system requested. Keep the original files, submission receipt, case number, and every response. Do not secretly record conversations without checking law and policy.</p>

<h2>Track deadlines and escalation</h2>
<p>Ask when acknowledgement and a decision should arrive and who handles an appeal or grievance. Follow up calmly in writing. Informal resolution usually does not pause a formal deadline unless the policy says so. Avoid retaliation yourself, and document any conduct you reasonably believe is retaliatory.</p>

<h2>Match an external route to the issue</h2>
<p>Possible routes include the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights for covered discrimination, the Student Privacy Policy Office for FERPA, Federal Student Aid’s Feedback Center for federal-aid matters, a state authorization or consumer-protection agency, an accreditor for compliance with accreditation standards, or a professional licensing body. These bodies have different jurisdiction and generally are not substitutes for a grade appeal. Your school must publish relevant state complaint contact information in covered circumstances.</p>

<h2>Get individualized help when stakes are high</h2>
<p>Consult legal aid or a licensed attorney for threatened removal, immigration consequences, criminal exposure, major financial loss, safety concerns, or a deadline you do not understand. A support person can help organize the record, but only an authorized decision maker can grant the requested remedy.</p>

<hr><h2>Official complaint resources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/file-complaint/how-file-discrimination-complaint-ocr">Office for Civil Rights: discrimination complaints</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/file-a-complaint">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA complaints</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/feedback-center/">Federal Student Aid Feedback Center</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/higher-education-laws-and-policy/college-accreditation">U.S. Department of Education: college accreditation</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. Filing in one place may not preserve another forum’s deadline.</em></p>
