        'Understanding Your School\'s Student Handbook' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A student handbook is a roadmap to institutional expectations, services, and procedures. It may contain the conduct code, complaint routes, academic-integrity rules, civil-rights contacts, safety policies, and appeal deadlines. It is rarely the only controlling document: the academic catalog, course syllabus, program manual, housing contract, employment policies, and specific case notice may also apply.</p>

<h2>Use the correct edition and scope</h2>
<p>Confirm the academic year, effective date, campus, delivery format, degree level, and student category. A graduate, online, professional-program, residential, or student-employee policy may differ from the general undergraduate handbook. Save a dated PDF or archived copy because webpages and policies change.</p>

<h2>Understand the document hierarchy</h2>
<p>Read any statement explaining which document controls a conflict. A syllabus can establish course details but may be subject to catalog and institution-wide rules. A department manual may add professional or progression requirements. An enrollment or housing agreement may create separate obligations. Ask the responsible office for written clarification rather than selecting the most favorable text yourself.</p>

<h2>Read definitions before procedures</h2>
<p>Terms such as “business day,” “notice,” “appeal,” “complaint,” “respondent,” “academic misconduct,” “harassment,” “withdrawal,” or “good standing” may have technical meanings. Determine whether a deadline begins when a decision is sent, received, or posted and whether weekends and breaks count.</p>

<h2>Bookmark the highest-impact sections</h2>
<ul>
  <li>academic integrity, grading, attendance, progression, suspension, dismissal, and appeals;</li>
  <li>student conduct, interim actions, hearing rights, sanctions, and appeals;</li>
  <li>Title IX, nondiscrimination, disability access, retaliation, and complaint contacts;</li>
  <li>FERPA, directory information, records access, and information releases;</li>
  <li>withdrawal, leave, refunds, aid, housing, safety, and emergency procedures.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Turn policy language into a checklist</h2>
<p>For any process, record the eligible grounds, required form, recipient, deadline, evidence rules, meeting or hearing steps, decision timeline, possible remedies, and next appeal. Follow linked policies because a handbook summary may omit detail. Keep submission receipts and written approvals; verbal advice may be incomplete.</p>

<h2>Know what the handbook cannot answer</h2>
<p>A handbook does not replace current federal or state law, professional licensing rules, individualized disability review, immigration advice, or the terms of a specific financial-aid program. Nor does every policy statement necessarily create the same legal rights at every school. For high-stakes interpretation, consult the policy owner, an ombuds office, legal aid, or counsel.</p>

<h2>Check required disclosures elsewhere</h2>
<p>Schools may publish FERPA notices, Clery security reports, financial-aid consumer information, accreditation, and state complaint contacts on separate webpages. Use the handbook as an index, then verify the current official source. If information conflicts, ask which version applies to your facts and preserve the response.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://fsapartners.ed.gov/knowledge-center/fsa-handbook/2025-2026/vol2/ch6-consumer-information-and-school-reporting">Federal Student Aid: consumer-information disclosures</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/faq/are-educational-agencies-and-institutions-required-notify-parents-and-eligible-students-their">Student Privacy Policy Office: annual FERPA notice</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security information</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. Use the current documents applicable to your campus, program, status, and event date.</em></p>
