        'Where to Go When You Don\'t Know Who Can Help' => <<<'HTML'
<p>When a college problem crosses offices, start with the office that owns the decision and ask for a “warm handoff”—the name, contact, form, and deadline for the correct next person. You do not need to know campus organization perfectly, but you should document referrals so a deadline is not lost while offices redirect you.</p>

<h2>Start with urgency</h2>
<p>For immediate danger call 911 or campus emergency services. For urgent medical or mental-health needs, contact emergency care or a crisis resource. For food, housing, transportation, or other basic-needs instability, contact the dean of students, student support, or basic-needs center. Administrative email is not emergency response.</p>

<h2>Academic and records questions</h2>
<p>Ask the instructor about course instructions and grading; an academic advisor about planning; the department chair or program director about program rules; and the registrar about registration, transcripts, enrollment, FERPA access, and degree records. For an appeal, locate the written policy before relying on informal advice.</p>

<h2>Money and enrollment questions</h2>
<p>Financial aid handles grants, loans, work-study, satisfactory academic progress, and aid effects. Student accounts or the bursar handles charges, payments, refunds, and balances. The registrar handles enrollment status, leave, and withdrawal records. Contact all three before changing enrollment because one office’s answer does not cover every consequence.</p>

<h2>Access, wellbeing, and discrimination</h2>
<p>Disability or accessibility services handles academic adjustments and auxiliary aids. Counseling and health services provide care within their scope. The Title IX coordinator handles sex-discrimination concerns; an equal-opportunity, civil-rights, or 504/ADA coordinator may handle other discrimination. Ask which resources are confidential and which employees must share reports.</p>

<h2>Conduct, safety, work, and special status</h2>
<p>Student conduct handles code allegations; campus safety or police handles safety and crime reporting; housing handles residence rules. Human resources or student employment handles workplace issues, though Title IX or civil-rights offices may overlap. International students should contact the designated school official before enrollment or employment changes. Veterans, athletes, graduate assistants, researchers, and licensed-profession students should also consult their specialized office.</p>

<h2>Use navigators and escalation</h2>
<p>The dean of students, student advocacy office, case manager, or ombuds office can help map a complicated problem. An ombuds office is often neutral, informal, and confidential within stated limits, but usually cannot replace a formal filing or extend a deadline. If the first contact cannot help, ask who owns the policy and request the referral in writing.</p>

<h2>Keep a small case file</h2>
<p>Save the applicable policy, names and dates, case numbers, forms, evidence, decisions, and a one-paragraph description of the requested outcome. Ask: “Is there a deadline I must preserve while we discuss this?” External civil-rights and FERPA complaints commonly have 180-day timing rules, so do not assume campus resolution stops an outside clock.</p>

<hr><h2>Official external starting points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/file-complaint/how-file-discrimination-complaint-ocr">Office for Civil Rights: complaint process</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/file-a-complaint">Student Privacy Policy Office: FERPA complaints</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/feedback-center/">Federal Student Aid Feedback Center</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/teaching-and-administration/safe-learning-environments/school-safety-and-security/campus-security">U.S. Department of Education: campus security</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. Emergency, school, state, and federal processes have separate roles and deadlines.</em></p>
